scope negative control — the WIRE identity seam (AC-9), scope.feature's headline claim.

Restores the FE-2824 defect the retarget proof exists to catch: the READ urls
address the SESSION's own client instead of the client the scope resolved. The
mutation hardwires `useActiveSession().useContext().activeUser` into the
collection's `loadList` url and the per-address `loadOne` url, inside the vehicle
module (`client-address.services.ts`) that scope's `resolveClientId` seam feeds.
The seam is left standing — `resolveClientId` still branches on `.for('client',
id)`, still feeds `queryKey`, `isAvailable` and every mutation — only the two
read urls stop consulting it. Right structure, gone capability.

Target spec:  scope.retarget-at-the-wire.int.test.ts
Should flip RED — the retarget READ read-back, in which the scope-resolved client
and the session's `activeUser` are DIFFERENT ids:
  "@AC-9 addresses the named client, as that client, with no acting-as header,
   while the session's own activeUser is a different client"

Stays GREEN, and this is the point:
  - "@AC-9 addresses the actor's own account when the scope names no entity" — one
    live actor whose session client IS the scope-resolved client, so "read the
    session" and "read the seam" resolve to the same id and a url assertion passes
    either way. That case is AC-9's ordinary-path coverage; it cannot detect the
    seam's removal on its own — which is exactly why the retarget case exists.
  - "@AC-9 sends the delete and the set-default to the named client, not the
    session's own" — the mutation is READ-only by construction (the write path
    still addresses the seam), so a red here can never be mistaken for a write
    regression.

